/* 1.4 — Fact or fiction: the myth as the guide lists it, and the fact from the guide's own chapters */
const MVLC_MYTHS=[
["Veterans only have one chance to use their VA home loan benefit.","Fiction. Entitlement is restored when the prior VA loan is paid in full and the home sold (basic restoration), once without selling (one-time restoration), and can be reused repeatedly. Eligibility never expires. (Ch.2 §2.11, §2.27)"],
["VA loans take too long to process and close.","Fiction. 2023 scorecard: average 18.8 days from VA case number to guaranty. Delays come from late COEs and appraisal ordering, not the program. (Ch.1 §1.3, Ch.2 §2.12)"],
["It's too difficult to qualify for a government program.","Fiction. VA has no minimum FICO in the Lender's Handbook, allows non-traditional credit, uses residual income instead of a hard DTI cap, and tells underwriters to use judgment and flexibility. (Ch.4 §4.26–4.27, §4.38)"],
["VA loans are too expensive with the upfront funding fee.","Fiction. The fee is financeable, can be paid by the seller within the 4% concession cap, drops with 5% or 10% down, and is waived for veterans receiving compensation for a service-connected disability, Purple Heart recipients on active duty, and eligible surviving spouses. No monthly mortgage insurance ever. (Ch.2 §2.28–2.30)"],
["Vets have to be discharged or retired to use their VA loan benefit.","Fiction. Active-duty members are eligible after 90 days (wartime) or 181 continuous days (peacetime); a Statement of Service is their proof. (Ch.2 §2.7, §2.13)"],
["Members of the Reserves or National Guard are not eligible.","Fiction. Six years of Reserve/Guard service with the required points and an honorable discharge, or release earlier for a service-connected disability, or qualifying Title 10 active duty. (Ch.2 §2.9)"],
["Vets serving away from home or overseas can't get a loan until they return to occupy.","Fiction. A spouse or dependent child satisfies occupancy for an active-duty veteran; deployed members are in temporary-duty status and meet occupancy; a POA can sign. (Ch.2 §2.35–2.36, Ch.4 §4.1)"],
["Widows or widowers of vets are not eligible for the VA loan benefit.","Fiction. The unmarried surviving spouse of a veteran who died in service or of service-connected causes, a surviving spouse who remarried at or after 57 on or after 12/16/2003, and the spouse of a member MIA/POW 90+ days are eligible. Entitlement codes 06 and 07. (Ch.2 §2.10, §2.15)"],
["VA loans require perfect credit scores.","Fiction. Not FICO-driven; Chapter 7 is 2 years from discharge, Chapter 13 is 1 year of on-time payments with trustee approval, isolated collections can be acceptable (5% of balance counted as a payment). (Ch.4 §4.26–4.34)"],
["The VA appraisal is a nightmare.","Fiction. VA relies on the sales-comparison approach like everyone else; Tidewater gives agents 48 hours to supply comps before a low value is final; ROV and appraiser-transfer rights protect the veteran; MPRs are safe, sound and sanitary, with field-office waivers possible. (Ch.5)"]];
